    "q-330574": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330574",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
